Consider neutrino-induced exclusive strangeness-production processes on nuclear targets at intermediate energies (incident neutrino energies of order 1–10 GeV), in which a strange hadron (e.g. a kaon or hyperon) is produced together with possibly other final-state particles. The measured cross section on a nucleus differs from the elementary neutrino–nucleon strangeness-production cross section because of three classes of nuclear effects:

(i) nuclear dynamics of the initial state, including the bound-nucleon momentum distribution, binding energy, Pauli blocking, and short-range correlations;
(ii) final-state interactions (FSI) of the produced strange and non-strange hadrons as they propagate through the residual nucleus, including elastic and inelastic rescattering, charge exchange, and absorption;
(iii) secondary production mechanisms in which strange hadrons are produced not in the primary neutrino–nucleon vertex but through subsequent hadronic interactions inside the nucleus (e.g. associated production by reinteracting pions or nucleons, hyperon–nucleon strangeness exchange).

Develop a quantitative model (analytic, Monte Carlo, or hybrid) of these three effects that is sufficiently reliable, with controlled and validated systematic uncertainties, that exclusive strangeness-production cross sections measured on nuclear targets can be unfolded to recover the underlying elementary neutrino–nucleon strangeness-production cross sections (such as ν_μ n → μ⁻ Λ K⁺, ν̄_μ p → μ⁺ Λ, ν̄_μ p → μ⁺ K⁻ p, etc.) with quantified accuracy. Specify the required theoretical inputs (spectral functions, hyperon– and kaon–nucleon interaction cross sections, in-medium modifications) and a procedure for extracting the elementary cross sections together with rigorous uncertainty estimates from nuclear-target data.

